  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe remnants of Isaias will continue to affect the area through \nMonday, with anomalously high moisture continuing to be pumped into \nthe region on strong southerly flow. Models continue to indicate a \npush of heavier precipitation tonight and again Sunday afternoon as \nthe mid and upper level portion of Isaias continues to push closer \nto the area. The low will move east across the area late tonight, \nwith a moist northwesterly flow taking hold behind the departing \nsystem, with precipitation and low clouds continuing to linger, \nparticularly across the mountainous counties for the first part of \nMonday. \n\nIn addition, although wind advisory criteria winds appear to have\nnot materialized across the mountains, will continue to maintain\nthe advisory due to numerous tree down reports received with\ncontinued gusty winds/wet ground. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure will then build in for early next week behind the \ndeparting low, with a brief break in the rainy weather, before \nanother frontal boundary affects the area mid week, providing for \nanother chance of light precipitation, followed by another system \nlate in the week. The late week system looks to carve a deep trough \nout across the eastern U.S., with colder weather taking hold across \nthe area. There could even be a few snow flakes across the higher \nterrain this weekend, depending on depth of moisture, but at this \npoint, accumulations are not anticipated.",
